{"name":"Transfusion","hex":"#ea1833","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"transfusion","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The transfer of blood or blood products from one individual to another.","The act of pouring liquid from one vessel to another."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/transfusion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blood transfusion","blocks":[{"text":"Blood transfusion is the process of transferring blood products into a person's circulation intravenously. Transfusions are used for various medical conditions to replace lost components of the blood. Early transfusions used whole blood, but modern medical practice commonly uses only components of the blood, such as red blood cells, plasma, platelets, and other clotting factors. White blood cells are transfused only in very rare circumstances, since granulocyte transfusion has limited applications. Whole blood has come back into use in the trauma setting."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Erythrozytenkonzentrat_neu.jpg/330px-Erythrozytenkonzentrat_neu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_transfusion"},{"title":"Platelet transfusion","blocks":[{"text":"Platelet transfusion, is the process of infusing platelet concentrate into the body via vein, to prevent or treat the bleeding in people with either a low platelet count or poor platelet function. Often this occurs in people receiving cancer chemotherapy. Preventive transfusion is often done in those with platelet levels of less than 10 billion/L. In those who are bleeding transfusion is usually carried out at less than 50 billion/L. Blood group matching is typically recommended before platelets are given. Unmatched platelets, however, are often used due to the unavailability of matched platelets. They are given by injection into a vein."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Platelet_blood_bag.jpg/330px-Platelet_blood_bag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platelet_transfusion"},{"title":"Transfusion (EP)","blocks":[{"text":"Transfusion is the second EP by the Australian rock group Powderfinger. It was released on 27 September 1993 by Polydor. The album was the group's first recording with Polydor, as the group had signed with the label due to the success of the self-titled previous EP by the band."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/68/Tranfussion_Front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfusion_(EP)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Blood_transfusion_B.jpg/500px-Blood_transfusion_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Blood_transfusion_B.jpg/1280px-Blood_transfusion_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Blood_transfusion_B.jpg/500px-Blood_transfusion_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A patient receiving blood transfusion through a cannula","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlood_transfusion_B.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Blood_bag_2020.jpg/500px-Blood_bag_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Blood_bag_2020.jpg/1280px-Blood_bag_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Blood_bag_2020.jpg/500px-Blood_bag_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Banked blood during the blood transfusion process","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlood_bag_2020.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Blood_unit_during_transfusion.jpg/500px-Blood_unit_during_transfusion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Blood_unit_during_transfusion.jpg/500px-Blood_unit_during_transfusion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A blood transfusion; the bag slowly empties, leaving behind blood that has clotted before it could be administered.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlood_unit_during_transfusion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Blausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png/500px-Blausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Blausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png/1280px-Blausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Blausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png/500px-Blausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration depicting intravenous blood transfusion","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0087_Blood_Transfusion.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/FreshFrozenPlasma.JPG/500px-FreshFrozenPlasma.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/FreshFrozenPlasma.JPG/1280px-FreshFrozenPlasma.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/FreshFrozenPlasma.JPG/500px-FreshFrozenPlasma.JPG?u